1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,984
Visitors read a site in their own language, and that starts with a list of cultures.

2
00:00:05,984 --> 00:00:10,802
In this video, you enable the Localization feature, choose the cultures your site

3
00:00:10,802 --> 00:00:13,017
supports, set a default, and save.

4
00:00:13,017 --> 00:00:15,620
Then you see a culture at work on a page.

5
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:23,817
A culture is a language, optionally with a region.

6
00:00:23,817 --> 00:00:28,570
French is one culture, and French as used in Canada is another, with

7
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,819
French as its parent culture.

8
00:00:30,819 --> 00:00:34,480
Each request to your site is served in one culture.

9
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:38,996
Your site keeps a list of supported cultures, and one of them is

10
00:00:38,996 --> 00:00:42,546
the default, the culture used when nothing asks for another.

11
00:00:42,546 --> 00:00:46,150
A site starts with only the culture of the system it runs on.

12
00:00:50,398 --> 00:00:53,334
Cultures belong to the Localization feature.

13
00:00:53,334 --> 00:00:56,328
Let's open Tools, Features in the admin menu.

14
00:00:58,373 --> 00:01:00,843
Type Localization in the search box.

15
00:01:00,843 --> 00:01:07,133
Several features match, so look for the row named Localization, in the Internationalization category.

16
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:09,330
Click Enable on that row.

17
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:12,569
A message says the feature Localization was enabled.

18
00:01:13,051 --> 00:01:17,603
Under Settings in the admin menu, a Localization entry has appeared.

19
00:01:17,603 --> 00:01:22,031
It holds Cultures, so let's open Settings, Localization, Cultures.

20
00:01:26,331 --> 00:01:28,179
The page is headed Settings.

21
00:01:28,179 --> 00:01:32,580
A warning says the website might be restarted upon saving the settings, and

22
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:36,601
a label asks you to define which cultures are supported by the site.

23
00:01:37,083 --> 00:01:40,121
The table is headed Culture and Default culture.

24
00:01:40,121 --> 00:01:43,891
It lists one culture, the one of the system the site runs on.

25
00:01:43,891 --> 00:01:48,223
Here it is English as used in the United States, marked as the default.

26
00:01:48,715 --> 00:01:52,263
To add a culture, choose it in the list under the table.

27
00:01:52,263 --> 00:01:57,527
Each entry shows the name of the culture, followed by its display name in parentheses.

28
00:01:57,527 --> 00:01:59,015
Let's choose French.

29
00:01:59,537 --> 00:02:02,389
Click Add culture, and French joins the table.

30
00:02:02,389 --> 00:02:06,063
A culture that is in the table no longer appears in the list.

31
00:02:06,063 --> 00:02:08,037
Let's add German the same way.

32
00:02:08,534 --> 00:02:12,326
Each row has a radio button in the Default culture column, and the

33
00:02:12,326 --> 00:02:14,117
selected one is the default.

34
00:02:14,117 --> 00:02:18,330
Let's select French to see the choice move, and then select English again,

35
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:20,174
so English stays the default.

36
00:02:20,662 --> 00:02:24,504
The trash button at the end of a row removes that culture from the table.

37
00:02:24,504 --> 00:02:28,346
If you remove the default culture, the first culture in the table becomes

38
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:29,902
the default when you save.

39
00:02:30,378 --> 00:02:34,151
Under the table, Fall back to parent culture(s) is a checkbox.

40
00:02:34,151 --> 00:02:37,923
When it is checked, a text with no translation in a culture is

41
00:02:37,923 --> 00:02:42,583
looked up in its parent culture, which for French as used in Canada is French.

42
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:43,748
Let's check it.

43
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:45,209
Click Save.

44
00:02:45,209 --> 00:02:47,375
The page reloads with a message:

45
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:49,711
Site settings updated successfully.

46
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:53,903
Saving restarts the site, so a warning stays on screen:

47
00:02:53,903 --> 00:02:57,926
The site has been restarted for the settings to take effect.

48
00:02:57,926 --> 00:03:02,254
The table keeps its three cultures, and the default is unchanged.

49
00:03:06,454 --> 00:03:10,253
A browser set to French asks for French, and the site answers with

50
00:03:10,253 --> 00:03:12,153
French texts when it has them.

51
00:03:12,153 --> 00:03:16,268
You can also ask for a culture yourself, by adding the culture parameter

52
00:03:16,268 --> 00:03:17,904
to the address of a page.

53
00:03:18,654 --> 00:03:21,216
The parameter applies to that request only.

54
00:03:21,216 --> 00:03:25,137
This site has a small sample file with French labels for the Cultures

55
00:03:25,137 --> 00:03:27,124
page, so you can see the effect.

56
00:03:28,712 --> 00:03:33,272
Let's open the Cultures page again, asking for French with the culture parameter.

57
00:03:33,758 --> 00:03:38,654
The heading, the column titles, the Add culture button, the checkbox label and

58
00:03:38,654 --> 00:03:40,927
the Save button appear in French.

59
00:03:40,927 --> 00:03:43,958
The table still lists the same three cultures.

60
00:03:44,462 --> 00:03:49,497
Opening the page without the parameter brings English back, because the browser asks

61
00:03:49,497 --> 00:03:51,902
for English, which the site supports.

62
00:03:56,102 --> 00:04:00,520
The list of cultures is the base that other localization features build on.

63
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:05,866
Content Localization manages localized versions of a content item in the supported cultures,

64
00:04:05,866 --> 00:04:09,902
and the Content Culture Picker offers the cultures on the front end.

65
00:04:10,652 --> 00:04:15,035
Data Localization lets you translate texts that the site stores, such as the

66
00:04:15,035 --> 00:04:18,082
names of content types, for each supported culture.

67
00:04:18,082 --> 00:04:21,503
The Admin Culture Picker offers the cultures in the admin.

68
00:04:21,503 --> 00:04:23,802
Separate videos cover these features.

69
00:04:24,552 --> 00:04:29,150
The Administrator role has the Manage supported culture permission by default.

70
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:33,693
A deployment plan can export the settings with the step Culture settings, and

71
00:04:33,693 --> 00:04:35,882
a recipe can set the cultures too.

72
00:04:37,882 --> 00:04:42,481
You enabled Localization, chose the supported cultures, set a default, and saw a

73
00:04:42,481 --> 00:04:44,192
culture at work on a page.

74
00:04:44,192 --> 00:04:45,582
Thanks for watching.

